\DOC LOCAL_SUB_P256_TAC

\TYPE {LOCAL_SUB_P256_TAC : int -> string list -> tactic}

\SYNOPSIS
Steps an {eventually} goal over an inlined modular-subtraction subroutine call,
advancing the symbolic state to a fresh successor state and replacing the
state-dependent assumptions with ones about that new state, plus a
specification of the subtraction result.

\DESCRIBE
The first argument, an {int}, selects a variant/offset of the subroutine
instance being stepped. The second, a {string list}, names in order the output
variable and the two input variables of the modular subtraction; these names
appear in the newly introduced assumption characterising the output in terms of
the two inputs modulo {p_256}, and determine which memory location is asserted
to hold the output. Applied to a goal whose conclusion is an {eventually}
predicate at a state variable {s_old}, the tactic produces one subgoal whose
conclusion has the same form but with a new, later state variable {s_new} in
place of {s_old}. Every assumption mentioning the old state — program-counter
equality, {bytes_loaded}, register reads, memory-content reads, and the
accumulated {MAYCHANGE} relation — is discharged and replaced by the
corresponding assumption about the new state; the {MAYCHANGE} component may
gain extra frame entries for the newly written location. The pure,
state-independent assumptions are retained (reordered), and one new such
assumption is added, saying that under range hypotheses on the two named inputs
the named output equals their difference modulo {p_256}; alongside it a
memory-read assumption records that the output location of the new state holds
the named output.
{
       A ?- eventually ... s_old
    ====================================  LOCAL_SUB_P256_TAC n [out; in1; in2]
       A' ?- eventually ... s_new
}
\noindent Here {A'} is {A} with the old state's assumptions replaced by the new
state's and the subtraction specification added.

\COMMENTS
All observed traces show this single kind of transformation, on goals of this
symbolic-execution shape.

\ENDDOC
